{
  "ncd": "110.14",
  "decision": "covered",
  "policy_quotes": [
    "Apheresis is covered for the following indications: Plasma exchange for acquired myasthenia gravis; Leukapheresis in the treatment of leukemia; Plasmapheresis in the treatment of primary macroglobulinemia (Waldenstrom); Treatment of hyperglobulinemias, including (but not limited to) multiple myelomas, cryoglobulinemia and hyperviscosity syndromes; Plasmapheresis or plasma exchange as a last resort treatment of thromobotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP); Plasmapheresis or plasma exchange in the last resort treatment of life threatening rheumatoid vasculitis; Plasma perfusion of charcoal filters for treatment of pruritis of cholestatic liver disease; Plasma exchange in the treatment of Goodpasture's Syndrome; Plasma exchange in the treatment of glomerulonephritis associated with antiglomerular basement membrane antibodies and advancing renal failure or pulmonary hemorrhage; Treatment of chronic relapsing polyneuropathy for patients with severe or life threatening symptoms who have failed to respond to conventional therapy; Treatment of life threatening scleroderma and polymyositis when the patient is unresponsive to conventional therapy; Treatment of Guillain-Barre Syndrome; and Treatment of last resort for life threatening systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) when conventional therapy has failed to prevent clinical deterioration.",
    "Apheresis is covered only when performed in a hospital setting (either inpatient or outpatient) or in a nonhospital setting, e.g., a physician directed clinic when the following conditions are met: A physician (or a number of physicians) is present to perform medical services and to respond to medical emergencies at all times during patient care hours; Each patient is under the care of a physician; and All nonphysician services are furnished under the direct, personal supervision of a physician."
  ],
  "missing_documentation": [],
  "rationale": "The patient meets the indications for apheresis, specifically plasmapheresis in the treatment of Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia. The apheresis clinic meets the requirements for coverage, with a physician on site to perform medical services and respond to emergencies, each patient under a physician's care, and the apheresis nurses working under the direct, personal supervision of the on-site physician."
}

{
  "ncd": "210.3",
  "decision": "covered",
  "policy_quotes": [
    "Medicare covers one screening FOBT per annum for the early detection of colorectal cancer. This means that Medicare will cover one gFOBT or one iFOBT at a frequency of every 12 months; i.e., at least 11 months have passed following the month in which the last covered screening FOBT was performed, for beneficiaries aged 45 years and older.",
    "Non-Invasive Biomarker tests are covered once every 3 years under the following conditions: - Ordering Criteria - When ordered by the physician, physician assistant, nurse practitioner, or clinical nurse specialist who will use the results in the management of the patient."
  ],
  "missing_documentation": [],
  "rationale": "The patient is 88 years old, which meets the age requirement for Medicare coverage of colorectal cancer screening. She has a family history of colon cancer, which is a risk factor for colorectal cancer. Her last screening FOBT was negative, but it was performed over a year ago, which meets the 12-month requirement for Medicare coverage. The nurse practitioner signs a written order for a screening iFOBT kit, which meets the ordering criteria for Medicare coverage. Therefore, the requested service is covered."
}
